| Reads cluster APIServer TLS security profile; enforces Intermediate profile as floor when unsupported version is requested | pkg/tls/profile.go:141-147 |\n371\t| **Crypto libraries** | stdlib crypto/tls (FIPS via OpenSSL with strictfipsruntime) | cmd/main.go:480-494 |\n372\t| **Certificate handling** | OpenShift service-ca provisioned TLS certificates for webhook and metrics endpoints | cmd/main.go:486-494 |\n373\t| **Non-FIPS crypto risks** | cloudmanager CGO_ENABLED uses build arg variable rather than explicit `1`; if built without CGO the binary would use pure-Go crypto | Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:59 |\n374\t\n375\tThe manager binary is built with explicit FIPS-compliant settings (`CGO_ENABLED=1`, `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime`), ensuring that Go's crypto operations delegate to OpenSSL on the host. The cloudmanager binary uses `-tags strictfipsruntime` but relies on a build argument for `CGO_ENABLED` rather than hardcoding it to `1`, which is a minor build-configuration risk if the argument is not provided. The operator reads the cluster-wide TLS security profile from the OpenShift APIServer and applies it to its own TLS configuration, ensuring alignment with platform TLS policy. [source: Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux:56-59, pkg/tls/profile.go:151-162]\n376\t## Admission Webhooks\n377\t\n378\t| Name | Type | Path | Port | Failure Policy | Resources | Operations | Purpose |\n379\t|----|----|----|----|--------------|---------|----------|-------|\n380\t| datascienceclusters.datasciencecluster.opendatahub.io | conversion | /convert |  |  | datascienceclusters | CONVERT |  |\n381\t| dashboard-acceleratorprofile-validator.opendatahub.io | validating | /validate-dashboard-acceleratorprofile |  | fail | acceleratorprofiles | CREATE, UPDATE |  |\n382\t| dashboard-hardwareprofile-validator.opendatahub.io | validating | /validate-dashboard-hardwareprofile |  | fail | hardwareprofiles | CREATE, UPDATE |  |\n383\t| datasciencecluster-v1-defaulter.opendatahub.io | mutating | /mutate-datasciencecluster-v1 |  | fail | datascienceclusters | CREATE, UPDATE |  |\n384\t| datasciencecluster-v1-validator.opendatahub.io | validating | /validate-datasciencecluster-v1 |  | fail | datascienceclusters | CREATE, UPDATE |  |\n385\t| datasciencecluster-v2-defaulter.opendatahub.io | mutating | /mutate-datasciencecluster-v2 |  | fail | datascienceclusters | CREATE, UPDATE |  |\n386\t| datasciencecluster-v2-validator.opendatahub.io | validating | /validate-datasciencecluster-v2 |  | fail | datascienceclusters | CREATE, UPDATE |  |\n387\t| dscinitialization-v1-validator.opendatahub.io | validating | /validate-dscinitialization-v1 |  | fail | dscinitializations | CREATE, DELETE, UPDATE |  |\n388\t| dscinitialization-v2-validator.opendatahub.io | validating | /validate-dscinitialization-v2 |  | fail | dscinitializations | CREATE, DELETE, UPDATE |  |\n389\t| podmonitor-injector.opendatahub.io | mutating | /mutate-prometheus-monitors |  | fail | podmonitors | CREATE, UPDATE |  |\n390\t\n391\t## Data Flows\n392\t\n393\t- **Admission and webhook flow:** The Kubernetes API server sends admission requests to the operator's webhook server on port 9443 (TLS via service-ca certificate `opendatahub-operator-controller-webhook-cert`). Mutating webhooks default DataScienceCluster v1/v2 resources, and validating webhooks enforce singleton constraints on DataScienceCluster and DSCInitialization resources. A mutating webhook injects monitoring labels into PodMonitor resources. [source: cmd/main.go:493-494, internal/webhook/datasciencecluster/v1/defaulting.go:22, internal/webhook/datasciencecluster/v1/validating.go:28]\n394\t- **Component reconciliation flow:** When a DataScienceCluster or DSCInitialization CR changes, the top-level controller reads the desired component states and triggers per-component controllers. Each component controller renders its manifests via kustomize/template actions, deploys them to the cluster via the Kubernetes API, and owns the resulting Deployments, Services, RBAC resources, and monitoring objects. [source: internal/controller/services/auth/auth_controller.go:57-87, internal/controller/services/gateway/gateway_controller.go:41-103]\n395\t- **Gateway ingress chain:** The gateway controller provisions a Gateway API chain: GatewayClass, Gateway, HTTPRoutes, and OpenShift Routes for external TLS-terminated access. A kube-auth-proxy deployment provides OIDC-authenticated access to the dashboard. On OpenShift clusters with integrated OAuth, an OAuthClient resource is created. The controller conditionally creates Istio EnvoyFilter and DestinationRule resources when detected. [source: internal/controller/services/gateway/gateway_controller.go:42-103]\n396\t- **Monitoring data flow:** The monitoring service controller deploys Prometheus proxies (cluster-proxy and namespace-proxy) with kube-rbac-proxy sidecars, a collector for MLflow trace export, and Routes for external Prometheus and Thanos querier access. All monitoring Routes use TLS. [source: internal/controller/services/monitoring/resources/data-science-prometheus-cluster-proxy.tmpl.yaml:59, internal/controller/services/monitoring/resources/data-science-prometheus-route.tmpl.yaml:1]\n397\t- **Security context:** 7 authentication rules and 11 secret references are documented in the Security section. The metrics endpoint enforces Kubernetes authentication and authorization when secure serving is enabled. The operator reads the cluster APIServer TLS security profile to configure its own TLS parameters. [source: cmd/main.go:483-485, pkg/tls/profile.go:151-162]\n398\t\n399\t## Integration Points\n400\t\n401\t- **/v1/ConfigMap:** Controller watch (Owns); protocol: Kubernetes API; purpose: componentHandler. [source: api/common/types.go:4, cmd/component-codegen/go.mod, cmd/main.go:29, 293, 493, 604, 608, cmd/manifest-tools/go.mod]\n402\t- **/v1/ConfigMap:** Controller watch (Owns); protocol: Kubernetes API; purpose: serviceHandler. [source: api/common/typ