f Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n\nThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence over the\nAI-generated Technical Approach. Overrides applied:\n\n1. **Controller architecture corrected to 5 tiers** — the module controller tier (8 module handlers: Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) is in scope; reflected in HLR6 and the implementation epic's scope.\n2. **Action file count corrected** — 15 action files across four tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module), not \"~16 component action files\"; the strategy body was already revised to match, and the epic scope uses the confirmed breakdown.\n3. **Strategy Open Question 5 (component action file count) resolved as answered** (8/8 components have exactly one action file) — no Investigation epic generated for it.\n4. **camelCase field naming** (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) adopted per SME input, matching controller-runtime conventions.\n5. **Cloudmanager uses the shared reconciler** and already receives `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger — only embedded-identity conversion and `resourceKind` are needed there.\n\n## Component Mapping\n\nCanonical Jira component `AI Core Platform` is used for both epics. The strategy's\naffected component is `rhods-operator` (manager + cloudmanager binaries), which does\nnot appear verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`; `AI Core Platform` is the\nclosest canonical name for the RHOAI platform operator. Both epics concern the\nrhods-operator codebase and its convention (the guideline documents the operator's\nown logging convention for controller development), so the same canonical component\napplies. The Cluster Observability Operator team performs no work in this strategy\n(publication is received at their discretion per platform policy overlay 0008), so no\nepic is assigned to them.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Cloudmanager release sequencing (non-bl