{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-c278fec7-a43f-4c15-85f1-1b358a53620d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-82447e1b9a2ae397","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8010-tmm99ph7/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8010-E001.md","content":"# Convert rhods-operator reconciliation error logs to structured resource context\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic converts message-embedded resource identity in rhods-operator reconciliation\nerror logs to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) across\nboth operator binaries (manager and cloudmanager), migrates the DSCInitialization\ncontroller from non-standard log field names to the standard camelCase convention,\nand adds a CI lint rule so the convention stays enforced for future controller code.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Audit** all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across both binaries, using grep/static analysis, and track coverage in a checklist. Manager binary scope (5 controller tiers):\n  - DataScienceCluster (DSC) controller and its action file (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`)\n  - 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) and their 8 component action files (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`)\n  - Module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) and 3 module action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`)\n  - DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller\n  - 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) and their 3 service action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`)\n  - Total action files: 15 (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module)\n- **Convert** every reconciliation error log call site that embeds resource name or namespace in the message string (e.g., `log.Error(err, \"failed to reconcile Foo in namespace bar\")`) to structured key-value pairs: `log.Error(err, \"reconciliation failed\", \"name\", foo.Name, \"namespace\", foo.Namespace, \"resourceKind\", \"Foo\")`.\n- **Migrate DSCI key names**: replace the non-standard structured field names `Request.Name` and `DSCInitialization` with the standard `name` / `namespace` keys.\n- **Add `resourceKind`** to reconciliation error logs in all controllers — required for service, module, and cloudmanager controllers (kind not implied by controller name) and added to DSC and component controllers for cross-controller queryability.\n- **Cluster-scoped resources** (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization): emit `name` and `resourceKind`; `namespace` omitted or empty — never a spurious value.\n- **Cloudmanager binary**: apply the same conversion to the AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, and CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine controllers (these use the shared reconciler and already receive `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger; they need embedded-identity conversion and `resourceKind`).\n- **CI lint / static analysis rule** flagging any `log.Error` call in reconciliation error paths that embeds name/namespace in the message text or is missing the required structured fields.\n- **Unit tests** asserting field presence and correctness via test logger output across controllers and all 15 action files.\n- **Log consumer regression validation**: before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error; deploy a test build to a dev cluster with the production log consumer stack (Loki, CloudWatch) configured, trigger reconciliation errors, and verify ingestion without configuration changes.\n- **SRE/Support coordination**: identify text-based alert rules that rely on the changed message-string patterns and update them before merge.\n- **Transition timing**: the DSCI key-name migration happens in a single release to avoid long-term inconsistency between old and new field names.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The CI lint/static analysis rule reports zero reconciliation-error `log.Error` call sites with name/namespace embedded in message text across the manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n- Unit tests assert that DSCI reconciliation error logs contain the standard field names (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), and a codebase grep confirms no remaining references to the old field names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) as log keys.\n- Unit tests assert that reconciliation error logs in all five service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) include `resourceKind` with the correct value.\n- Unit tests assert that error logs in all 15 action files across the four tiers (8 component, 3 service, 1 DSC, 3 module) include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` with correct values.\n- Unit tests assert that module controller error logs across all 8 module handlers include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`.\n- Unit tests assert that the three cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`.\n- Unit tests assert that DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` (cross-controller queryability).\n- Unit tests assert that cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization) emit `name` and `resourceKind` and do not emit a spurious `namespace` value.\n- A before/after comparison of a sample reconciliation error's log output confirms the structured fields appear, and the dev-cluster validation with the production log consumer stack confirms both old and new formats are ingested without configuration changes.\n- Text-based alert rules relying on the changed message-string patterns are identified with SRE/Support and updated before merge.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- [P0] All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) — covered.\n- [P0] DSCI controller migrated from non-standard key names to camelCase convention — covered.\n- [P0] All service controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` — covered.\n- [P0] All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across all 4 tiers (15 action files) — covered.\n- [P0] All cloudmanager controller reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` — covered.\n- [P0] Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) — covered.\n- [P1, bundled] DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` for cross-controller queryability — bundled into this epic: it edits the exact same `log.Error` call sites as the P0 conversion pass (same surface, same mechanical edit); splitting would double-touch every call site and duplicate the audit, so it is not separately schedulable.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- **Inconsistent adoption**: some error paths may be missed across the 5 controller tiers and 15 action files — mitigated by the grep/static-analysis audit, a coverage checklist, and the CI lint rule added in this epic.\n- **Log consumer regression**: log consumers (Loki, CloudWatch, Elasticsearch) or text-based alerts may break when message strings change — mitigated by the dev-cluster ingestion validation and SRE/Support alert-rule coordination in scope.\n- **DSCI query breakage**: operators with existing queries filtering on `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` will need the migration documented — handled by the convention documentation work that follows this epic; the transition completes in a single release.\n- **Cloudmanager release sequencing** (open question, PM): whether the cloudmanager controller changes ship in the same release as the manager changes is a scheduling decision; the work itself is identical either way. If a subsequent release is chosen, the cloudmanager scope should be split into its own epic, keeping the audit tooling and lint rule shared.\n- The audit confirming the actual percentage of message-embedding log calls (assumed 75%) is the first step of this epic; the scope — all call sites embedding identity — is invariant to the result.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":54573,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-978424004576674a","agent_id":"adde2c5938b2627c5","session_id":"2909a9de-3cce-4cc2-ac2e-bb90c9612f5b","uuid":"fd2d55da-22b6-4cbb-82eb-0e2026f98dc5","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:27:59.521Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8010 into epics"}
