{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-96aee7ed4a84f74d","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\ttitle: Operator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context\n4\tstatus: New\n5\tpriority: Major\n6\tlabels:\n7\t- observability\n8\t- refined\n9\tlinks:\n10\t- 'source-rfe: RHAIRFE-7119'\n11\t- 'related-strategy: RHAISTRAT-7055'\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## Business Need (from RFE)\n15\t\n16\t> The full business need is maintained in the source RFE: RHAIRFE-7119\n17\t\n18\t## Strategy (AI Generated by Agentic SDLC Pipeline)\n19\t\n20\t### TL;DR\n21\t\n22\tConvert message-embedded resource identity to structured key-value pairs across rhods-operator reconciliation error logs. Audit shows 75% of log calls embed name/namespace in unstructured message strings (e.g., `log.Error(err, \"failed to reconcile Foo in namespace bar\")`); these must be converted to `log.Error(err, \"reconciliation failed\", \"name\", foo.Name, \"namespace\", foo.Namespace, \"resourceKind\", \"Foo\")`. DSCI controller uses non-standard key names (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`) and must be migrated to camelCase convention (`name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind`). Framework controllers (DSC, 8 component controllers) already emit `name`/`namespace` via controller-runtime's request logger at the reconcile entry point; missing piece is adding `resourceKind` for queryability. Scope includes manager binary (8 component controllers, 8 component action files, 5 service controllers with 3 service action files, DSC controller with 1 DSC action file, module controller with 8 module handlers and 3 module action files, DSCI controller) and cloudmanager binary (multi-cloud infrastructure controllers using the shared reconciler). Total action files: 15. For external operators (COO: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator), publish the convention for voluntary adoption per platform policy.\n23\t\n24\t**Components**: rhods-operator (manager + cloudmanager binaries)\n25\t\n26\t### Technical Approach\n27\t\n28\tThe rhods-operator uses controller-runtime's logr-based structured logging. An audit of reconciliation error paths reveals three distinct problems, not one:\n29\t\n30\t**Problem 1: Message-embedded identity (75% of log calls)**. Most reconciliation error logs embed resource name and namespace in unstructured message strings (e.g., `log.Error(err, \"failed to reconcile Dashboard 'example-dashboard' in namespace 'redhat-ods-applications'\")`) rather than as structured key-value pairs. This makes the logs human-readable but prevents operators from querying by resource name or namespace. The fix: convert these to `log.Error(err, \"reconciliation failed\", \"name\", dashboard.Name, \"namespace\", dashboard.Namespace, \"resourceKind\", \"Dashboard\")`.\n31\t\n32\t**Problem 2: DSCI uses non-standard key names**. The DSCInitialization controller uses `Request.Name` and `DSCInitialization` as structured field names, diverging from controller-runtime's camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`). This breaks cross-controller log queries. The fix: migrate DSCI to the standard convention.\n33\t\n34\t**Problem 3: resourceKind never added**. While framework controllers (DSC and the 8 component controllers) already emit `name` and `namespace` via controller-runtime's request logger at the reconcile entry point, none of the controllers add `resourceKind` as a structured field. For component controllers reconciling a single kind, `resourceKind` is somewhat redundant (the reconciler name already implies the kind), but it enables cross-controller queries like \"show all reconciliation errors for kind=DataScienceCluster\" without parsing reconciler names. For module handler action files and service controllers, adding `resourceKind` is critical because these controllers do not reconcile a single kind. The fix: add `resourceKind` to all reconciliation error logs.\n35\t\n36\t**Controller architecture**: The rhods-operator manager binary has five controller tiers: (1) the DataScienceCluster (DSC) controller, (2) 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI), (3) the module controller with 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator), (4) the DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller, and (5) 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup). The DSC controller manages component enablement. Each component controller has a paired module in `components/<name>/` with a `<name>_controller_actions.go` file containing component-specific action functions invoked during reconciliation. These component action files contain reconciliation logic but are not separate controllers — they are invoked by the component controller's reconcile loop. The module controller's action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`) handle module lifecycle logic. The DSCI controller manages platform initialization and dependency installation. Service controllers handle cross-cutting platform services; three (Auth, Gateway, Monitoring) have paired action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`).\n37\t\n38\tThe **cloudmanager** binary ships with the rhods-operator and handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies (deployed on AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). It has its own reconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern (these controllers already get `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger). The same three problems apply: message-embedded identity in error paths, no `resourceKind`, and (if applicable) non-standard key names.\n39\t\n40\t**Implementation scope**: Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across both binaries. For each call site embedding resource identity in the message string, extract the identity fields and convert to structured KV pairs. Add `resourceKind` to all controllers. Migrate DSCI from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace`. Action files across all four tiers are in scope — these contain reconciliation logic and emit error logs. Total scope: 8 component action files (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`) + 3 service action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`) + 1 DSC action file (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`) + 3 module action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`) + 8 component controllers + module controller + DSC controller + DSCI controller + 5 service controllers + 3 cloudmanager controllers. Total action files: 15.\n41\t\n42\tField naming uses camelCase (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) to align with controller-runtime's existing structured log key conventions (Staff Engineer / SME Input). Required fields: `name` and `namespace` (P0); `resourceKind` (P1 for component controllers, P0 for service/module/cloudmanager controllers where kind is not implied by controller name).\n43\t\n44\t**External operators**: For COO components (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) referenced in the RFE, publish the convention as a specification for voluntary adoption. Per platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies. The RFE approval comment explicitly confirms this approach.\n45\t\n46\t### Affected Components\n47\t\n48\t* **rhods-operator** (ODH Operator team): Convert message-embedded resource identity to structured KV pairs, add resourceKind field, and migrate DSCI to standard key names across all controller tiers (DSC, 8 component controllers, module controller, DSCI, 5 service controllers, 3 cloudmanager controllers, and 15 action files across 4 tiers: 8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module)\n49\t\n50\t### Impacted Teams\n51\t\n52\t* **ODH Operator team** (rhods-operator): Audit error-path log calls across all 5 controller tiers and 15 action files, convert message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs, add resourceKind, migrate DSCI key names from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace`\n53\t* **Cluster Observability Operator team** (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator): Receive published convention — adoption at their discretion per platform policy\n54\t\n55\t### High Level Requirements\n56\t\n57\t* \\[P0\\] All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`)\n58\t* \\[P0\\] DSCI controller migrated from non-standard key names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) to camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`)\n59\t* \\[P0\\] All service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` as a structured field (kind is not implied by controller name for these controllers)\n60\t* \\[P0\\] All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields across all 4 tiers: 8 component action files (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`), 3 service action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`), 1 DSC action file (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`), 3 module action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`)\n61\t* \\[P0\\] All cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields\n62\t* \\[P0\\] Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields (8 module handlers: Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator)\n63\t* \\[P1\\] DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` for cross-controller queryability (kind is implied by controller name, so this is queryability enhancement rather than functional requirement)\n64\t* \\[P1\\] Document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption\n65\t\n66\t### Dependencies\n67\t\n68\t* **controller-runtime logr interface** (internal, exists): Provides `logr.Logger` with structured key-value pair support — all changes build on this existing capability\n69\t* **COO team adoption** (external, unknown): Loki/Tempo Operator log format changes depend on external team prioritization; per platform policy (overlay 0008), RHOAI cannot mandate or manage this\n70\t\n71\t### Non-Functional Requirements\n72\t\n73\t* **Backwards Compatibility**: Log format changes are additive — structured fields are added alongside existing log messages. Log message strings will change (e.g., removing redundant resource identity from message text after moving it to structured fields), but the addition of structured key-value pairs and message text changes must not break existing log ingestion pipelines. Verification method: Before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error — confirm structured fields appear and log consumer (Loki, CloudWatch) ingests both formats without configuration changes. Coordinate with SRE/Support to identify and update any text-based alert rules that rely on message string patterns. (RFE: scope states this is log standardization, not log overhaul)\n74\t* **Performance**: Structured logging via logr adds negligible overhead. No performance targets specified in RFE or architecture context. Verification method: No verification required — controller-runtime's logr is production-proven infrastructure with known negligible overhead characteristics. (architecture context: rhods-operator already uses logr throughout its controller hierarchy)\n75\t\n76\t### Out-of-Scope\n77\t\n78\t* Comprehensive operator log overhaul across all 26+ RHOAI operator components — scope limited to reconciliation error logs (RFE: explicitly stated)\n79\t* Inference gateway logging (covered by separate RFE)\n80\t* Log collection pipeline configuration (covered by separate RFE)\n81\t* Custom log dashboards or alerting rules (RFE: explicitly excluded)\n82\t* Non-reconciliation log paths (info, debug, warning logs outside reconciliation error flows)\n83\t\n84\t### Acceptance Criteria (Proposed — requires PM/Engineering validation)\n85\t\n86\t* Given a reconciliation error log call in any rhods-operator controller that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string (e.g., `log.Error(err, \"failed to reconcile Dashboard 'example' in namespace 'redhat-ods-applications'\")`),\n87\t\n88\t  when the code is updated,   then the log call uses structured key-value pairs instead (e.g., `log.Error(err, \"reconciliation failed\", \"name\", \"example\", \"namespace\", \"redhat-ods-applications\")`),   measured by CI lint rule or static analysis flagging any remaining `log.Error` calls with name/namespace embedded in message text.\n89\t\n90\t* Given the DSCI controller's reconciliation error logs,\n91\t\n92\t  when the code is updated,   then all structured field names use camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) instead of non-standard names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`),   measured by unit test asserting DSCI error logs contain the standard field names and grepping the codebase confirms no references to the old field names remain.\n93\t\n94\t* Given a service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) encounters a reconciliation error,\n95\t\n96\t  when the error is logged,   then the log entry includes `resourceKind` as a structured field,   measured by unit test parsing test logger output and asserting `resourceKind` is present with the correct value.\n97\t\n98\t* Given an action file error log across any of the 4 tiers (8 component: `components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`, 3 service: `services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`, 1 DSC: `datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`, 3 module: `modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`),\n99\t\n100\t  when the code is updated,   then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields,   measured by unit test asserting all three fields are present with correct values across all 15 action files.\n101\t\n102\t* Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error,\n103\t\n104\t  when the error is logged,   then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields,   measured by unit test asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n105\t\n106\t* Given a cluster-scoped resource (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization) is reconciled and encounters an error,\n107\t\n108\t  when the error is logged,   then the log entry includes `name` and `resourceKind` as structured fields, and `namespace` is either omitted or set to empty string,   measured by unit test confirming cluster-scoped resources do not emit a spurious namespace value.\n109\t\n110\t* The structured log field convention is documented in a contribution guideline specifying required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing resourceKind).\n111\t\n112\t### Effort Estimate\n113\t\n114\t**S (Small, 1-2 sprints).** The logging infrastructure (logr, controller-runtime) already exists and framework controllers already emit `name`/`namespace`. Primary effort: (1) audit `log.Error()` call sites across all 5 controller tiers including cloudmanager and 15 action files (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) (\\~0.5 sprint), (2) convert message-embedded resource identity to structured KV pairs, add `resourceKind` to all controllers including the module controller and its 8 module handlers, and migrate DSCI key names from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace` (\\~0.75-1 sprint), (3) fix cloudmanager controllers (\\~0.25 sprint — these use the shared reconciler so already have name/namespace, just need resourceKind + convert embedded identity), (4) document convention and add CI lint rule (\\~0.5 sprint).\n115\t\n116\t### Risks\n117\t\n118\t* **Inconsistent adoption across controllers**: With 8 component controllers, module controller (8 module handlers), 5 service controllers, 3 cloudmanager controllers, DSC, DSCI, and 15 action files across 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module), some error paths may be missed during the initial audit (especially in the action files which contain tier-specific reconciliation logic) — _Mitigation_: Use grep/static analysis to identify all `log.Error` call sites; track coverage in a checklist; add a CI lint rule to flag new `log.Error` calls missing required structured fields.\n119\t* **Log consumer regression**: Existing log consumers (Loki, CloudWatch, Elasticsearch) or log-based alerting rules may fail to ingest logs with the new structured fields, or text-based alerts may break when message strings change (e.g., resource identity removed from message text after moving to structured fields) — _Mitigation_: Before merge, deploy a test build to a dev cluster with the production log consumer stack configured, trigger reconciliation errors, and verify logs are ingested correctly and existing alert queries still match; coordinate with SRE/Support to identify and update any text-based alert rules that rely on message string patterns.\n120\t* **External operator adoption delay**: COO team may not prioritize log convention adoption — _Mitigation_: Publish the convention early and file tracking issues in COO projects; accept that external adoption is best-effort per platform policy (overlay 0008).\n121\t* **DSCI key name migration breaks existing queries**: Operators may have existing Loki/CloudWatch queries filtering on the non-standard `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` field names — _Mitigation_: Document the migration in release notes and provide before/after query examples; ensure the transition happens in a single release to avoid long-term inconsistency.\n122\t\n123\t### Assumptions\n124\t\n125\t* controller-runtime's logr interface supports adding arbitrary structured key-value pairs to log calls — confirmed (architecture context validates controller-runtime/logr usage throughout rhods-operator)\n126\t* Framework controllers (DSC, 8 component controllers, module controller) already emit `name` and `namespace` via controller-runtime's request logger at the reconcile entry point — confirmed (Staff Engineer / SME Input)\n127\t* 75% of reconciliation error log calls embed resource identity in message strings rather than structured fields — needs validation (audit required to confirm percentage and identify affected call sites)\n128\t* All 15 action files across 4 tiers contain reconciliation error logs — confirmed (Staff Engineer / SME Input: 8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module)\n129\t\n130\t### Open Questions\n131\t\n132\t* **Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind (e.g., reconciliation attempt count, error category)?** _(Owner: PM / Observability team)_ — additional fields increase utility but also log volume\n133\t* **Which existing log-based alerts or dashboards rely on message-string patterns that will change?** _(Owner: SRE / Support)_ — this must be identified during the log consumer regression validation step (see Risks) before merge\n134\t* **Should cloudmanager controllers be prioritized for the same release as manager controllers, or can they follow in a subsequent release?** _(Owner: PM / ODH Operator team)_ — cloudmanager is only deployed on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave), so impact scope is narrower than manager\n135\t* **What is the actual percentage of log calls that embed resource identity in message strings?** _(Owner: ODH Operator team)_ — SME input says 75%, but this needs confirmation via codebase audit\n136\t\n137\t### Supporting Documentation\n138\t\n139\t* Source RFE: RHAIRFE-7119\n140\t* Related observability outcome: RHAISTRAT-7055\n141\t* Parent RFE (archived): RHAIRFE-7084\n142\t* Architecture context: rhods-operator component doc (rhoai-3.5-ea.2) — confirmed controller-runtime/logr usage, confirmed cloudmanager binary scope, validated action file architecture and exact counts\n143\t* Platform policy overlay: 0008 — No external operator auto-install policy\n144\t* Staff Engineer / SME Input: Corrected controller architecture to 5 tiers (8 component controllers, module controller with 8 module handlers, DSC, DSCI, 5 service controllers), confirmed exact action file breakdown (15 total: 8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module), diagnosed three distinct problems (message-embedded identity, DSCI non-standard keys `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`, missing resourceKind), specified camelCase field naming convention, noted cloudmanager uses shared reconciler and already gets name/namespace\n145\t* Design doc: \\_To be created\\_ — should document the three-problem diagnosis, provide before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs, and include DSCI key migration guide\n146\t\n147\t### Prerequisites & Process Gates\n148\t\n149\t* **Architecture Review**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n150\t* **ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n151\t* **Licence Validation**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n152\t* **Accelerator/Package Support**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n153\t* **Documentation Support**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n154\t* **UXD Support**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n155\t* **Performance Team Support**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n156\t\n157\t## Staff Engineer / SME Input\n158\t\n159\t1. \"\\~16 component action files\" is wrong — and mislabeled\n160\t\n161\tThere are exactly 8 component action files (one per component, confirmed by glob). The broader codebase has 15 total action files across four tiers, but calling them all \"component\" is inaccurate:\n162\t\n163\tTier\tAction files\tCount Component controllers components/<name>/<name>\\_controller_actions.go 8 Service controllers services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/_\\_controller_actions.go 3 DSC controller datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go 1 Module controller modules/modules_controller_actions_.go 3 Total 15\n164\t\n165\t1. Module controller tier is missing from the architecture description\n166\t\n167\tLine 90 describes \"four controller tiers\" but omits the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator). The module controller's action files DO have error logs — modules_controller_actions.go has log.Error() calls (e.g., \"BuildModuleCR failed\", \"DAG reverse resolution failed\") and modules_controller_actions_inject_env.go has log.Error() calls with structured fields. These are not covered by any of the four listed tiers.\n168\t\n169\tLine 88 mentions \"module handler action files\" in passing but the architecture description, HLRs, and scope don't include them.\n170\t\n171\t1. Open Question 5 is based on a wrong premise\n172\t\n173\t\"How many component action files exist across the 8 component controllers? — SME input suggests \\~16 files total (not all 8 components have action files)\"\n174\t\n175\tAll 8 components DO have exactly one action file (confirmed: 8/8). This question is already answered and can be removed or replaced with a note stating the confirmed count.\n176\t\n177\tConfirmed Correct Three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, DSCI non-standard keys, missing resourceKind) DSCI key names: \"Request.Name\" for name, \"DSCInitialization\" for namespace — matches dscinitialization_controller.go:154,169,182 8 component controllers correctly listed 5 service controllers correctly listed including Setup Cloudmanager uses shared reconciler (reconciler.ReconcilerFor) — already gets name/namespace External operator treatment aligns with RFE approval comment and platform policy Cluster-scoped resource AC (line 164-167) is a good addition Effort estimate S is reasonable Backwards compatibility and log consumer regression risks are well described camelCase convention for structured log keys matches controller-runtime practice\n178\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af6848a0cefe5aaa","agent_id":"a825e9fcfe71f443e","session_id":"2909a9de-3cce-4cc2-ac2e-bb90c9612f5b","uuid":"2a4e280f-23d9-4971-8a4b-cd22f59d6cfa","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:29:40.928Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-96aee7ed4a84f74d","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
