{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4cb3925b86a176e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n3\ttitle: Convert rhods-operator reconciliation error logs to structured resource context\n4\t  (name/namespace/resourceKind)\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n6\tcomponent: AI Core Platform\n7\tteam: ODH Operator team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 1\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: 1\n17\t  external_dependency: 1\n18\t  human_process_gates: -1\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 6\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Convert rhods-operator reconciliation error logs to structured resource context\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic converts message-embedded resource identity in rhods-operator reconciliation\n35\terror logs to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) across\n36\tboth operator binaries (manager and cloudmanager), migrates the DSCInitialization\n37\tcontroller from non-standard log field names to the standard camelCase convention,\n38\tand adds a CI lint rule so the convention stays enforced for future controller code.\n39\t\n40\t## Scope\n41\t\n42\t- **Audit** all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across both binaries, using grep/static analysis, and track coverage in a checklist. Manager binary scope (5 controller tiers):\n43\t  - DataScienceCluster (DSC) controller and its action file (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`)\n44\t  - 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI) and their 8 component action files (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`)\n45\t  - Module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) and 3 module action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`)\n46\t  - DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller\n47\t  - 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) and their 3 service action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`)\n48\t  - Total action files: 15 (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module)\n49\t- **Convert** every reconciliation error log call site that embeds resource name or namespace in the message string (e.g., `log.Error(err, \"failed to reconcile Foo in namespace bar\")`) to structured key-value pairs: `log.Error(err, \"reconciliation failed\", \"name\", foo.Name, \"namespace\", foo.Namespace, \"resourceKind\", \"Foo\")`.\n50\t- **Migrate DSCI key names**: replace the non-standard structured field names `Request.Name` and `DSCInitialization` with the standard `name` / `namespace` keys.\n51\t- **Add `resourceKind`** to reconciliation error logs in all controllers — required for service, module, and cloudmanager controllers (kind not implied by controller name) and added to DSC and component controllers for cross-controller queryability.\n52\t- **Cluster-scoped resources** (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization): emit `name` and `resourceKind`; `namespace` omitted or empty — never a spurious value.\n53\t- **Cloudmanager binary**: apply the same conversion to the AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, and CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine controllers (these use the shared reconciler and already receive `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger; they need embedded-identity conversion and `resourceKind`).\n54\t- **CI lint / static analysis rule** flagging any `log.Error` call in reconciliation error paths that embeds name/namespace in the message text or is missing the required structured fields.\n55\t- **Unit tests** asserting field presence and correctness via test logger output across controllers and all 15 action files.\n56\t- **Log consumer regression validation**: before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error; deploy a test build to a dev cluster with the production log consumer stack (Loki, CloudWatch) configured, trigger reconciliation errors, and verify ingestion without configuration changes.\n57\t- **SRE/Support coordination**: identify text-based alert rules that rely on the changed message-string patterns and update them before merge.\n58\t- **Transition timing**: the DSCI key-name migration happens in a single release to avoid long-term inconsistency between old and new field names.\n59\t\n60\t## Acceptance Criteria\n61\t\n62\t- The CI lint/static analysis rule reports zero reconciliation-error `log.Error` call sites with name/namespace embedded in message text across the manager and cloudmanager binaries.\n63\t- Unit tests assert that DSCI reconciliation error logs contain the standard field names (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), and a codebase grep confirms no remaining references to the old field names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) as log keys.\n64\t- Unit tests assert that reconciliation error logs in all five service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) include `resourceKind` with the correct value.\n65\t- Unit tests assert that error logs in all 15 action files across the four tiers (8 component, 3 service, 1 DSC, 3 module) include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` with correct values.\n66\t- Unit tests assert that module controller error logs across all 8 module handlers include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`.\n67\t- Unit tests assert that the three cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind`.\n68\t- Unit tests assert that DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` (cross-controller queryability).\n69\t- Unit tests assert that cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization) emit `name` and `resourceKind` and do not emit a spurious `namespace` value.\n70\t- A before/after comparison of a sample reconciliation error's log output confirms the structured fields appear, and the dev-cluster validation with the production log consumer stack confirms both old and new formats are ingested without configuration changes.\n71\t- Text-based alert rules relying on the changed message-string patterns are identified with SRE/Support and updated before merge.\n72\t\n73\t## HLR Traceability\n74\t\n75\t- [P0] All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) — covered.\n76\t- [P0] DSCI controller migrated from non-standard key names to camelCase convention — covered.\n77\t- [P0] All service controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` — covered.\n78\t- [P0] All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across all 4 tiers (15 action files) — covered.\n79\t- [P0] All cloudmanager controller reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` — covered.\n80\t- [P0] Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) — covered.\n81\t- [P1, bundled] DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` for cross-controller queryability — bundled into this epic: it edits the exact same `log.Error` call sites as the P0 conversion pass (same surface, same mechanical edit); splitting would double-touch every call site and duplicate the audit, so it is not separately schedulable.\n82\t\n83\t## Risks / Notes\n84\t\n85\t- **Inconsistent adoption**: some error paths may be missed across the 5 controller tiers and 15 action files — mitigated by the grep/static-analysis audit, a coverage checklist, and the CI lint rule added in this epic.\n86\t- **Log consumer regression**: log consumers (Loki, CloudWatch, Elasticsearch) or text-based alerts may break when message strings change — mitigated by the dev-cluster ingestion validation and SRE/Support alert-rule coordination in scope.\n87\t- **DSCI query breakage**: operators with existing queries filtering on `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` will need the migration documented — handled by the convention documentation work that follows this epic; the transition completes in a single release.\n88\t- **Cloudmanager release sequencing** (open question, PM): whether the cloudmanager controller changes ship in the same release as the manager changes is a scheduling decision; the work itself is identical either way. If a subsequent release is chosen, the cloudmanager scope should be split into its own epic, keeping the audit tooling and lint rule shared.\n89\t- The audit confirming the actual percentage of message-embedding log calls (assumed 75%) is the first step of this epic; the scope — all call sites embedding identity — is invariant to the result.\n90\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b14d9112af375c77","agent_id":"adb32d634ca5409be","session_id":"2909a9de-3cce-4cc2-ac2e-bb90c9612f5b","uuid":"588a12cb-9b41-4ef2-8181-4e4100a9ac2d","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:33:00.860Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4cb3925b86a176e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
