{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a47962275a81ef70","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 2\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\tOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage Note\n15\t\n16\tStep 0 below-threshold was evaluated and rejected on the multiple-distinct-work-streams\n17\tescape condition: the strategy spans (a) mechanical code implementation (converting\n18\t`log.Error` call sites to structured key-value pairs, DSCI key migration, CI lint rule)\n19\tand (b) substantive content authoring (a new contribution guideline specifying the\n20\tconvention, a DSCI key migration guide, release-notes documentation, and publication\n21\tfor external operator adoption — P1 HLR). These are different work products requiring\n22\tdifferent skills and review cycles, so the strategy decomposes into code and\n23\tdocs-authoring epics. No genuine unknown changes which epics exist (see Open Question\n24\tDisposition below), so no Investigation epics were generated.\n25\t\n26\t## Epic List\n27\t\n28\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n29\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Convert rhods-operator reconciliation error logs to structured resource context (name/namespace/resourceKind) | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n32\t\n33\t## Dependency DAG\n34\t\n35\t```mermaid\n36\tgraph TD\n37\t    E001[\"E001 — Structured reconciliation log conversion (manager + cloudmanager)\"]\n38\t    E002[\"E002 — Convention guideline and publication\"]\n39\t    E001 --> E002\n40\t```\n41\t\n42\t## DAG Justification\n43\t\n44\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n45\t|------|------|-----------|\n46\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | The contribution guideline documents the convention as implemented — required fields, before/after code examples drawn from the converted call sites, and the DSCI key migration guide — so it must be authored after the implementation lands. Coupling is one-directional: the implementation does not depend on the document. |\n47\t\n48\tNo other edges: the implementation is one logical change (a single mechanical audit-and-convert\n49\tpass over the `log.Error` reconciliation-error surface of one repo, one team), and there are no\n50\trepo/konflux/license onboarding chains (rhods-operator is an existing, Konflux-onboarded repo per\n51\tarchitecture context `Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux`).\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n54\t\n55\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n56\t|-----|----------|------|-------|\n57\t| All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs embedding resource name/namespace in message strings are converted to structured KV pairs (`name`, `namespace`) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | |\n58\t| DSCI controller migrated from non-standard key names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) to camelCase convention | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | |\n59\t| All service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | |\n60\t| All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across all 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module action files) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | |\n61\t| All cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | |\n62\t| Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | |\n63\t| DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` for cross-controller queryability | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | **bundled** — not separately schedulable: it edits the exact same `log.Error` call sites in the same controllers and action files as the P0 conversion pass (same surface, same mechanical edit); splitting would double-touch every call site and duplicate the audit. Bundled at the epic's P0 priority. |\n64\t| Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Substantive content authoring — distinct work-product stream (Rule: work-product type boundaries). |\n65\t\n66\tFull coverage: all 8 HLRs map to epics; no orphaned requirements.\n67\t\n68\t## Open Question Disposition (Step 3 — no Investigation epics)\n69\t\n70\t| Open question | Owner | Disposition |\n71\t|---------------|-------|-------------|\n72\t| Should the convention include fields beyond name/namespace/kind (attempt count, error category)? | PM / Observability | Does not change which epics exist or what they do — the HLRs fix the required fields; additional fields would be additive content in the guideline. Captured as an open question on the docs epic (extension pending PM decision). |\n73\t| Which existing log-based alerts/dashboards rely on message-string patterns that will change? | SRE / Support | Validation activity, not a structural unknown — folded into the implementation epic as an acceptance criterion (identify and update affected alert rules before merge), per the strategy's own risk mitigation. |\n74\t| Should cloudmanager ship in the same release as manager, or follow in a subsequent release? | PM / ODH Operator | Scheduling decision — the cloudmanager conversion work exists and is identical either way. See Health Warnings for the split trigger. |\n75\t| What is the actual percentage of log calls embedding resource identity? | ODH Operator | Diagnostic figure only — the scope is defined by \"all call sites that embed identity\", which is invariant to the percentage. The audit (grep/static analysis) is step 1 of the implementation epic. |\n76\t\n77\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides (Step 1.5)\n78\t\n79\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence over the\n80\tAI-generated Technical Approach. Overrides applied:\n81\t\n82\t1. **Controller architecture corrected to 5 tiers** — the module controller tier (8 module handlers: Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator) is in scope; reflected in HLR6 and the implementation epic's scope.\n83\t2. **Action file count corrected** — 15 action files across four tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module), not \"~16 component action files\"; the strategy body was already revised to match, and the epic scope uses the confirmed breakdown.\n84\t3. **Strategy Open Question 5 (component action file count) resolved as answered** (8/8 components have exactly one action file) — no Investigation epic generated for it.\n85\t4. **camelCase field naming** (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) adopted per SME input, matching controller-runtime conventions.\n86\t5. **Cloudmanager uses the shared reconciler** and already receives `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger — only embedded-identity conversion and `resourceKind` are needed there.\n87\t\n88\t## Component Mapping\n89\t\n90\tCanonical Jira component `AI Core Platform` is used for both epics. The strategy's\n91\taffected component is `rhods-operator` (manager + cloudmanager binaries), which does\n92\tnot appear verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`; `AI Core Platform` is the\n93\tclosest canonical name for the RHOAI platform operator. Both epics concern the\n94\trhods-operator codebase and its convention (the guideline documents the operator's\n95\town logging convention for controller development), so the same canonical component\n96\tapplies. The Cluster Observability Operator team performs no work in this strategy\n97\t(publication is received at their discretion per platform policy overlay 0008), so no\n98\tepic is assigned to them.\n99\t\n100\t## Health Warnings\n101\t\n102\t- **Cloudmanager release sequencing (non-blocking)**: Open question (PM) on whether cloudmanager controllers ship in the same release as manager controllers. The implementation epic bundles both binaries because it is one mechanical pass over one repo with a shared audit and lint rule. **Re-split trigger**: if PM decides cloudmanager follows in a subsequent release, split the cloudmanager controller scope (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) out of the implementation epic into its own epic — the lint rule and audit tooling must then be shared across both.\n103\t- **E001 effort-based re-split trigger (non-blocking)**: the implementation epic sits at or slightly above the ~2-week guideline (~1.5-2 sprints of the strategy's own S estimate: 0.5 audit + 0.75-1 conversion + 0.25 cloudmanager + lint-rule share). It is one mechanical pass over one surface in one repo, so a split would duplicate the audit and fragment the change; the overage is a managed trade-off. **Re-split trigger**: if the audit reveals materially more `log.Error` call sites than estimated, or the effort outgrows a sprint, split along binary (manager/cloudmanager) or controller-tier lines, keeping the audit tooling and lint rule shared across the resulting epics.\n104\t- **P1 HLR bundled into P0 epic**: HLR7 (resourceKind for DSC and component controllers) is bundled with the P0 conversion pass — see the inline `bundled` flag in the HLR Traceability Matrix. It is a queryability enhancement over the same call sites, not a deferrable feature on a separate surface.\n105\t- **Externally-controlled validation gate**: the log-consumer regression check (dev-cluster deployment with the production log consumer stack) and the SRE/Support alert-rule update depend on teams outside the ODH Operator team; they are acceptance criteria on the epic's deliverable (the validation evidence and updated alert rules), but their scheduling is not controlled by this decomposition.\n106\t- **Strategy maturity**: the strategy's acceptance criteria are marked \"Proposed — requires PM/Engineering validation\" and all prerequisites (Architecture Review, Build Onboarding, License Validation, etc.) are pending human review. These are process gates on the strategy, not decomposition unknowns.\n107\t\n108\t## Tiered Delivery\n109\t\n110\tNot applicable. There is no external dependency gating delivery: the COO team's\n111\tadoption of the convention (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) is voluntary and explicitly\n112\toutside RHOAI's control per platform policy overlay 0008 — the strategy delivers full\n113\tvalue through publication alone, modeled as an acceptance criterion on the docs epic\n114\t(tracking issues filed), not as a gated Tier 2.\n115\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd180cce1c2c6989","agent_id":"af3aa444b2ea17eed","session_id":"2909a9de-3cce-4cc2-ac2e-bb90c9612f5b","uuid":"da13aa50-58fb-4737-b4f5-765ccbc64e2c","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:34:05.666Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a47962275a81ef70","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
